Here's the CartComponent that renders a list of items, allows quantity adjustments, and removes items when clicked. This assumes Redux is configured like the previous code snippet:

```jsx
const CartComponent = () => {
  const dispatch = useDispatch();
  const cart = useSelector(state => state.cart.items);
  const [checkoutOpen, setCheckoutOpen] = useState(false);

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={() => setCheckoutOpen(true)}>Checkout ({cart.reduce((acc, i) => acc + i.quantity, 0)})</button>
      <div style={{ minHeight: 300 }}>
        {cart.map((item, index) => (
          <div key={index}>
            {item.name}
            <button onClick={() => dispatch(removeItem(item.id))}>Remove</button>
            Quantity:
            <select
              value={item.quantity}
              onChange={e => {
                dispatch(addItem({...item, quantity: parseInt(e.target.value) }));
                dispatch(removeItem(item.id));
              }}
            >
              {[...Array(10)].map((_, index) => (
                <option key={index} value={index + 1}>{index + 1}</option>
              ))}
            </select>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <Modal open={checkoutOpen} onClose={() => setCheckoutOpen(false)}>
        <p>
          Total: $<b>{cart.reduce((acc, i) => acc + i.price * i.quantity, 0)}</b>
        </p>
      </Modal>
    </div>
  );
};
```

This component uses `useDispatch` and `useSelector` (both from `react-redux`) to connect to your Redux store, and `useState` to store the state of the checkout modal. The JSX renders the list of items with quantity inputs and remove buttons, a checkout button, and the checkout modal itself, which displays the total for the contents of the cart.

